---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { rooms, roomWorks, wings } from '../lib/exhibitions';
import { storyPages, storyPath, workFacts } from '../lib/story';
const total = rooms.reduce(
  (n, room) => n + roomWorks(room).length + room.relatedItems.length,
  0,
);
const withPost = [...workFacts.values()].filter((f) => f.post).length;
---

<Layout
  title="Notes from the curator — The nnxyz Rooms"
  description="How the rooms, the gallery text, the story pages, the audio tour and the map of this collection were made."
>
  <article class="text-page notes-page">
    <h1>Notes from the curator</h1>
    <p>
      The rooms of this site are unsigned, like the wall text in a museum. This
      page is the exception: a few notes on how the collection was hung and
      written, and on what the text does and does not claim.
    </p>

    <h2>Who the curator is</h2>
    <p>
      The curator is an AI assistant working in conversation with the collector.
      The assistant looked at every work, proposed the rooms, wrote the text,
      the captions and the story pages, and read its own drafts back in page
      order for repetition. The collector set the brief, answered the questions
      the hanging raised, approved the structure and the pilot room, chose the
      voice for the audio tour, and wrote the statement on the front page. Those
      are the only words of the collector's on the site, apart from his
      published writing quoted on the story pages. Everything else on the walls
      was written by the assistant from the works, their archived records, the
      public ledgers and the artist's own posts, and corrected where a second
      look showed it wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      One person is named on the site: the collector, Jamie Thingelstad, whose
      wallets, purchases and writing are part of the story. No one else in his
      household is named or described anywhere, in the rooms, the story pages or
      the records behind them. Other buyers appear by their Ethereum names where
      they have one, and are otherwise counted, not named.
    </p>

    <h2>How the rooms were hung</h2>
    <p>
      Each collection is a wing. New Nifty on the Block, the largest at 303
      works, is split into three dated parts because the drawing changes as the
      year goes on. Inside each wing the works were laid out on dated contact
      sheets and grouped by what they look like, not by their titles or numbers:
      black silhouettes with wavy marks, heads built from boxes, masks buried in
      scribble. Every work hangs in exactly one room, rooms hold twenty to
      thirty works, and each room is hung in the order the works were drawn, so
      a walk through a room is also a walk through a few weeks or months of
      drawing. Two exceptions were the collector's decisions: the two Rawblocks
      landscapes hang as a coda rather than being forced into a room, and one
      greeting by Scrimshaw hangs as a related work at the end of Occasions and
      Figures. Each wing ends on its collection piece where the series has one.
      The {wings.length} wings include After Nifty Ink, the artist's later work
      on other chains, hung in two rooms from the collector's holdings.
    </p>

    <h2>How the text was written</h2>
    <p>
      The voice is a museum label's: specific, restrained, and about what is on
      the wall. A label says where the eyes are and what the beam does to the
      scribble under it, and then says what the picture is doing and what it
      feels like, as a reading stated plainly. Where the text reads a feeling or
      a message in the work, it is grounded in the picture, the archived record
      and the artist's own sourced words: the titles, the bio lines, the
      contract names, the posts. No quotation, biography or influence is
      invented; comparisons to other artists are the gallery's, not the
      artist's, and are made plainly. The market is named where it touches a
      room, with the date. Stock praise and repeated metaphors were cut in an
      editing pass over all {rooms.length} rooms; so were most of the numbers,
      which belong in the details panel of each work.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every number that remains comes from software reading the archive and the
      public ledgers, not from looking: stroke counts, dates, the order of
      works, prices, the day a work reached the collector. Dates and times are
      UTC, from the archived creation timestamps, because this is a gallery for
      visitors in every time zone; the artwork pages add your local time beside
      the UTC instant. Where two works share a title, or one is a fork of
      another, the text follows the stable drawing identifiers rather than the
      names.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rooms were written one at a time, each after the assistant had looked
      at every work in the room at full size and as one wall. One room, Black
      Silhouettes, was written first as a pilot and shown to the collector
      before the rest. The text was then read as a whole, in page order, and
      edited for the tics that creep into {rooms.length} rooms written in a few
      days; a second pass rewrote every room to carry the story, once the
      research into the ledgers and the artist's posts was done.
    </p>

    <h2>The story pages and the facts on the walls</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/story/">five story pages</a> are the gallery's, written in
      the same voice from the archive, the public ledgers, the artist's posts
      and the collector's published writing. Each page lists its sources at the
      bottom. The collector's words are quoted and dated; the artist's are
      quoted verbatim from posts saved by id, with three light edits (links and
      hashtag-only lines dropped, handles written as names, emoji removed) and
      nothing paraphrased. The year 2022 is laid out month by month with the
      counts beside the prose.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two facts were added to every Nifty Ink artwork page from the same
      research. The details panel says when the work reached the collector and
      how, from the first transfer of the edition into one of his wallets:
      bought from the artist, sent with a complete set, or minted into his
      wallet by the artist. And where the artist's own post announcing the
      drawing was found, {withPost} works in all, the page prints it with its
      date, the price the artist asked and a link to the original.
    </p>

    <h2>The drawings themselves</h2>
    <p>
      The Nifty Ink drawings are reconstructed from their archived brush
      strokes, so each one is shown as a native vector image and can be watched
      being drawn, stroke by stroke, with the same order, widths, colors and
      transparency the archive records. The archive does not record how fast the
      artist drew, so playback timing is reconstructed and labelled as such. The
      Abstract works survive only as published images from the collector's
      earlier gallery; they are shown as stills, with their original files
      available to download. The later works on other chains are shown from
      their original files, with a link to the animation where there is one. The
      complete archived records remain downloadable from every artwork page.
    </p>

    <h2>The audio tour, the screen saver and the map</h2>
    <p>
      The gallery text was written in segments tied to works so that it could
      also be heard. Each room's segments were read by a synthetic voice chosen
      by the collector from samples, with a short list of spoken forms for names
      that are written one way and said another. The tour brings each work on
      screen as its segment plays; silent works hold for a few seconds. The
      screen saver shows one work at a time with the standalone caption every
      work was given. The map draws the collection as a building on three
      floors, with rooms sized by how many works they hold and a door from each
      room into the next, which is how a visitor would walk it.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site is not</h2>
    <p>
      It is not a catalogue of either artist's complete work; it shows the{' '}
      {total} works in one collection, and{' '}
      <a href="/catalogue/">the catalogue</a> lists every collection the artist
      is known to have made, held here or not. It is not biography: what it says
      about the artist is what the artist has said and done in public, on the
      board, on the chains and on X, and the story pages cite each of those
      records. And it is not a verdict on quality. Measurements such as stroke
      count describe how a drawing was recorded, not how good it is or how long
      it took.
    </p>
    <p>
      If something on a wall is wrong, it can be corrected: every reading is
      kept as a record with the image it was written from, and a correction
      replaces the text without touching the archive.
    </p>
    <p>
      {storyPages.map((page, i) => (
        <>
          <a href={storyPath(page.slug)}>{page.title}</a>
          {i < storyPages.length - 1 ? ' · ' : ''}
        </>
      ))}
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/about/">About the collection →</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/">Return to the collection →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
